8. **Title:** The economist who was terrified of AI just found a rare reason for hope  
   **Topic:** economy  
   **URL:** https://fortune.com/2026/04/19/alex-imas-human-jobs-ai-economy-chicago-economist-substack-doomsday-scenario/  
   **Published At:** 2026-04-19  
   **Source:** Fortune  
   **Article Content:**  
   University of Chicago economist Alex Imas, a leading researcher on AI’s labor market impact, has proposed a vision of how the AI economy could avoid a "demand-collapse" doomsday scenario. Imas argues that as AI makes commodity production and technical tasks cheaper and more abundant, economic value will migrate toward what he calls the “relational sector.” This encompasses roles where human presence, social connection, and provenance are the primary drivers of value—such as nurses, teachers, therapists, and even baristas.  

   Imas cites the "Starbucks signal" as a primary example: despite having the technology to automate stores for years, Starbucks recently pivoted back to "human details"—handwritten notes, ceramic mugs, and more baristas—because human connection proved more valuable than efficiency. Drawing on structural change theory and the concept of "mimetic desire," Imas posits that as people get richer through AI-driven efficiencies, they will demand goods and services with high "income elasticity," specifically human-to-human interactions that cannot be commodified.  

   The framework suggests that many existing jobs won’t disappear but will transform. As AI automates routine workday tasks for doctors or teachers, the emotional support and relationship-building components will become the core of the job's economic value. However, Imas warns that this optimistic "hopeful version" of the economy depends entirely on the speed of transition; if automation accelerates faster than workers and institutions can reallocate, the risk of high unemployment and a drag on global growth remains severe.  

